course: arts
lesson: 35
title: Copying vs being inspired: tracing, covers, giving credit
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every artist learns by copying: tracing a fox from a book, singing a famous song, drawing a cartoon character, or painting a copy of a masterpiece in a gallery.
explain: Copying to learn is fine; the problem starts when you pass off someone else's work as your own, like entering a traced picture in a competition or pasting a website's sentences into homework.
explain: Being inspired is different from copying: you take an idea, a mood or a style, like the gold patterns of the Austrian painter Gustav Klimt, and make a new picture of your own cat with it.
explain: The fair rule is simple: make it your own, and say who or what inspired you, the same way you would want your name on a drawing someone shared online.
explain: People say it their own way: "numpty" means silly person in Scotland and the north, "da" is a friendly "mate" in Chennai, "eish" is a South African "wow" or "oh no", and "chur" in New Zealand means thanks or cheers.
fact: Tracing is a common way to learn shapes and lines; it becomes a problem only if you claim it as your own original drawing, for example in a competition.
fact: A cover is a new performance of someone else's song; covers are normal, and when they are released, the songwriters are named and paid.
fact: Facts are free to use, but someone's exact words are their writing; copying sentences without credit is called plagiarism.
fact: Fan art means drawing characters from shows, games or books you love; sharing it for fun and saying where the character comes from is usually accepted.
fact: The Louvre lets registered artists copy paintings in its galleries, with rules about size so the copies cannot pass as the originals; selling a copy as the real thing is forgery, a crime.
fact: Songs often share a few notes or chords, because music uses a limited number of notes; whether two songs are too alike is argued, sometimes in court.
fact: There is no "change 30 percent and it's yours" rule; what matters is whether the original can still be recognised.
fact: The first Mickey Mouse, from the 1928 cartoon Steamboat Willie, became free for anyone to use in the United States in 2024, but later Mickeys and Disney's brand are still owned.
fact: Van Gogh painted his own versions of prints by the Japanese artist Hiroshige to learn from them, and that influence changed his style.
fact: Posting someone's art without their name takes away their credit; artists often sign their work, and sites let them report reposts.
fact: Being inspired means using an idea or style in something new; copying means taking the actual picture.
exercise: Sophie traced a fox and hung it on the fridge. Is that cheating? | No, tracing to practise is fine | Yes, tracing is always cheating, even when it is only for practice at home | Yes, because pictures on a fridge must always be original drawings | No, foxes belong to nobody
exercise: Arnav sings a famous song at a concert. What is it called? | A cover | Plagiarism | Forgery | Fan art
exercise: What happens when a cover song is released for sale? | The songwriters are named and paid | The singer becomes the new owner | Nobody has to be named | The song stops belonging to anyone
exercise: Taylor copies a paragraph about Frida Kahlo and says facts belong to nobody. What is true? | The facts are free, the wording is not | She is right, any sentence that is about facts is free for anyone to copy | Copying whole paragraphs is fine as long as it is for art homework | Websites own no words
exercise: What is plagiarism? | Passing off others' work as yours | Singing a cover of a famous song at a school concert | Drawing a cartoon character you love in your own style | Tracing a picture at home to practise drawing shapes
exercise: Ji-woo's fan art is called "theft". Which is fair? | Crediting the show, it is usually fine | All fan art is stealing, even if it is drawn in your own style | Fan art is against the law in every country of the world | Fan art is for adults only
exercise: Matteo sees a man copying a painting in the Louvre. Is it illegal? | No, registered artists may copy | Yes, copying any painting at all is a crime in every country | Yes, unless he paints with his eyes closed so it is not exact | Yes, unless he is famous
exercise: When does copying a painting become forgery? | When it is passed off as the real one | When the copy is very good and looks almost exactly like the original | When it is painted inside a gallery instead of at home | When a child paints it
exercise: Saoirse hears two songs with the same few notes. What is true? | Shared notes are common | Any two songs that sound a bit alike have definitely been stolen | Only one song in the whole world is allowed to use those notes | Songs with the same few notes count as exactly the same song
exercise: Bohdan's friend says, "Change thirty percent and it's yours." What is true? | There is no such rule | It is the law in every country, written in the rules for artists | You only need to change ten percent, not thirty, to make it yours | The real rule is fifty percent, so he needs to change a lot more
exercise: Lily says old Mickey is free, so she can make her own Disney movie. What is true? | Only the 1928 Mickey is free | Everything Disney has ever made became free for everyone in 2024 | Mickey can never be used by anyone, in any version, for ever | Only Disney songs are free
exercise: Thandi thinks Van Gogh was a fraud because he copied Japanese prints. What is true? | He learned from them openly | He secretly sold the Japanese prints as his own paintings for money | Great artists never learn anything from other artists' work | He was jailed for it
exercise: Kai's drawing is posted by someone else with no name. What is fair to do? | Report it, ask for credit | Nothing, because any art that is online belongs to everyone | Post their art without their names too, so it is fair | Stop drawing for ever so nobody can take his art again
exercise: Ana paints her cat in Klimt's gold style. Is she a copycat? | No, it is a new picture | Yes, any picture with gold patterns in it is a copy of Klimt | Yes, because she used a famous painter's idea without paying for it | No, but only because Klimt never painted any cats himself
exercise: What is being inspired? | Using an idea or style to make something new | Taking the actual picture and signing it | Tracing a drawing for a competition | Selling a copy as the real thing
exercise: What is the honest way to use facts from a website? | Your own words, plus the source | Copy the sentences exactly as they are without saying where from | Change one word in each sentence so it is not the same any more | Use only the pictures
test: Your mate goes, "Singing someone else's song is stealing, innit." What is the best answer? | Covers are normal | He is right, nobody is allowed to sing anybody else's songs | Only famous singers are allowed to do covers, not school kids | Covers are only allowed when you sing quietly in the shower
test: What would happen if Sophie entered her traced fox in a drawing competition as her own? | It would be dishonest, because she claimed it as original | It would be fine because she coloured it | She would win because tracing is skill | Nothing, competitions allow any tracing
test: A kid says "that's literally theft, cringe" about fan art. What is a fair reply? | Credited fan art is usually fine | He is right, fan art is always illegal and people get arrested for it | Fan art is only allowed in Korea and Japan, not anywhere else | Only creators may draw it
test: In a cartoon, a boy changes the hat on the Mona Lisa and sells it as his own painting. What is wrong? | It is still clearly the same picture, passed off as his | Nothing, a new hat makes it new | The Mona Lisa is not a real painting | It is fine if he sells it cheaply
test: A Gen Z kid says, "Old Mickey's free now, so all Disney is free, slay" ("slay" means awesome). What is the correction? | Only the 1928 Mickey is free | She is right, everything Disney has made is now free to use | Nothing about Mickey is free, not even the very oldest cartoon | Only the Disney songs are free, the characters are not
test: Thandi says "eish, Van Gogh copied" (eish means oh no). What is the fair view? | Learning from others openly is how art grows | Van Gogh should not be famous | Copying is always fraud | He hid where his ideas came from
test: Your friend copies three sentences about Frida Kahlo word for word for homework. What would be the honest fix? | Rewrite it in her own words | Change the font and colour so that it looks different from the website | Add one sentence of her own at the end so the teacher cannot tell | Use another website
test: Kai says "chur, they stole it and got 10k likes" (chur here means cheers, said sarcastically). Which is true? | He keeps the right to credit | Once any art is posted online, it belongs to anyone who finds it | All the likes go to the real artist automatically anyway | Likes go to the artist
test: A Limerick girl says two pop songs are "a robbery, grand" because of a few shared notes. What is fair? | Similar notes are common, so it is not always copying | Any shared note proves theft | Songs must never share any notes | Only old songs may share notes
test: Ana's friend shouts "Copycat!" at her Klimt-style cat. What is the difference Ana means? | Style borrowed, picture new | There is no difference at all between being inspired and copying | Being inspired means that you traced the picture very carefully | Copying is fine as long as you add a cat to the famous picture
test: A man in a gallery paints a copy of a famous painting, smaller than the original. Why the size rule? | So his copy cannot pass as the real painting | So it fits in his bag | Because big paintings are not allowed | So the original does not get jealous
exam: Identify the principal circumstance in which the tracing of a drawing becomes dishonest. | Where it is presented as original | Where the tracing is performed for the purposes of practice at home | Where the tracing is subsequently coloured by the person who traced it | Where it is displayed at home
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises plagiarism? | The presentation of another person's words or work as one's own | The performance of a song written by another person | The making of fan art with credit to its source | The use of freely available facts in one's own words
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the earliest Mickey Mouse. | Only the 1928 version became free in the US in 2024 | Every version of the character became free for public use in 2024 | No version of the character may ever be used by the public in any form | Only post-2024 versions are free
exam: Ascertain the distinction between being inspired by a work and copying it. | Inspiration makes a new work; copying reproduces | Inspiration and copying are identical in every respect and in law | Copying is permitted wherever a work is altered by thirty percent | Inspiration is for professionals
